        "falling debris": "hit",
        "alarm pulses": "retriever_gap",
        "illusory walls": "retriever_gap"
      },
      "stale_hits": [],
      "global_stale_hits": [],
      "answer": "TL;DR: The council room actively interferes via arcane traps (damage/paralysis), illusion effects (walls/disorientation), environmental hazards (falling debris), and security systems (rune alarms + amulet-based targeting).\n\nThe room itself is hostile infrastructure, not just a backdrop:\n\n- **Arcane trap bursts (CANON/OBSERVED via Players & Guards):** These can deal **2d6 force damage** and **temporarily paralyze** targets. Importantly, **Players are targeted if they lack amulets**, while **Guards are exempt due to amulets** (CANON: Players defenses; Guards relationship_tags).\n\n- **Illusions (CANON \u2013 Players mental_state):** The space uses **illusory walls/illusion spells**, leaving players **disoriented** and potentially mispositioned mid-fight.\n\n- **Falling debris (OBSERVED \u2013 Players physical_condition):** The chamber creates **forced movement/pressure via debris hazards**, requiring dodging and breaking positioning.\n\n- **Rune alarm system (OBSERVED \u2013 Guards operational_status):** **Rune alarms alert guards**, escalating the fight and bringing reinforcements or tightening control.\n\n- **Ambient signal (CANON \u2013 city portrayal_notes / Players portrayal_notes):** The **wall hum changes** during key events\u2014this can telegraph trap triggers or phase shifts.\n\nKey twist: the room distinguishes friend/foe via **amulets**, meaning the environment selectively punishes the party while protecting aligned guards.\n\nKey Attributes:\n- Defenses: Arcane traps (damage, paralysis), amulet-based targeting, rune alarms  \n- Terrain Effects: Illusions, falling debris  \n- Sensory Signals: Shifting wall hum indicating changes or triggers",
      "embedding_similarity": null
    },
    {
      "id": "q_the_emergency_council_meeting_2_v2",
      "question": "Can we trust Thalia in this emergency meeting, or is something off?",
      "strict_verdict": "fail_incomplete",
